Install the IPsec tunnels again after a P-CSCF restart into a new namespace.

Patch 0015 keeps the tunnels across a restart that keeps the network
namespace. One that does not - a container recreated, a pod replaced - starts
with no SAs at all, while the preloaded contacts, and the UEs, still hold
theirs: everything the UEs send over ESP is dropped until they re-register.

The contacts now carry everything the tunnel was built from (CK, IK, the
negotiated algorithms, both sides' SPIs and ports, the UE's address), so
ipsec_restore_tunnels() calls create_ipsec_tunnel() for each of them - both,
for a contact holding the tunnel it is registered over and an offer beside it. The adds
use NLM_F_EXCL, so an SA or policy that survived the restart makes its add fail
and stays as it is, counters and all; only what is missing is created. One path
covers both kinds of restart, and a tunnel left half-installed as well.

A put-back outbound SA must not start numbering at 1. The UE's inbound SA has
seen the old one's sequence numbers and drops what it has seen and everything
more than its replay window (32) below the highest, so a fresh SA would be
silent towards the UE until it caught up - for a UE that received a thousand
packets, a thousand lost MT requests and responses. Nothing records how far the
old SA got, so the new ones start above a bound instead: add_sa() takes the
starting oseq (XFRMA_REPLAY_VAL), and the put-back outbound SAs start at
ipsec_restore_oseq_rate (default 16) per second since 2026-01-01. That holds as
long as no SA sends faster than the rate on average, and still holds for a
second restart while a UE is on a put-back SA; it runs out of 32-bit headroom in
about 8 years at the default, and is clamped with a warning before then.
Inbound SAs need nothing: a fresh replay state accepts the UE's numbers.

The replacement has to come up on the same ipsec_listen_addr: the UE's SAs and
policies are bound to it.

